  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Tuesday...\n\nMixed westerly winds have become breezy today, as expected, with\ngusts 25-35 mph extending eastward through Miles City & Baker.\nHighest gusts will remain along western foothills and our north.\nHarlowton recently gusted to 43 mph. Look for widespread temps in\nthe 70s this afternoon, with thickest high cloud cover with a\nchinook arch to our north.\n\nSatellite imagery shows fast zonal flow across SW Canada. An\ninitial wave is pushing thru central AB, while another wave is\nnear the BC coast. The latter wave will track closer to the MT\nborder tonight into Wednesday, and be associated with a Canadian\nfront's arrival tomorrow afternoon. As a result, breezy winds \ncontinue Wednesday with a shift to NW-NE winds through the day. \nTemps tomorrow will be several degrees cooler than today but still\na little warmer than normal. The fropa itself will be dry.\n\nBackdoor cooling/moistening is expected Wednesday night and\nThursday. There may be enough overrunning mid level moisture to\nproduce ripples of light showers Wednesday night through Thursday\nnight, mainly over our north and east, and in fact most recent \nmodels have trended slightly wetter here. The deepening/moistening\nlow level upslope flow may also yield low stratus and/or fog at \nsome point, and this will be monitored. Temps should drop to the \nupper 50s to mid 60s for highs Thursday, with cooler (but not\ncold) nights.\n\nBrief/flat ridging and return to downslope winds should push temps\nback to the mid 60s to mid 70s Friday. Another Canadian wave and\ncold front arrives Friday night, setting up a modestly cooler \nSaturday. This next Canadian front looks drier but still cannot\nrule out a brief period of light showers over our north and east.\nIn its wake, a more amplified ridge builds over the PacNW and\nnorthern Rockies. There is high confidence in a higher height \nregime from the weekend thru the first part of next week. Though \nthe ridge axis to our west suggests additional periods of backdoor\ncooling are possible, the strong ridge supports above normal \ntemps and dry wx through the period, with perhaps lower 80s \nreturning next Monday/Tuesday. \n\nThere is high confidence for warmer & drier than normal conditions\npersisting in the 8-14 day time frame (Oct 7-13), per a stagnant \nridge over the western CONUS. The Pacific tropics remain very \nactive and it may take a tropical storm/hurricane to alter the \nlongwave pattern at some point.\n\nJKL",
